## Order Creation Saga — Orchestration

```
SAGA STEPS (happy path):
1. Create Order (OrderService)       → status: PENDING
2. Reserve Inventory (InventoryService) → items reserved
3. Process Payment (PaymentService)  → charged
4. Schedule Shipping (ShippingService) → scheduled
5. Confirm Order (OrderService)      → status: CONFIRMED

COMPENSATING TRANSACTIONS (if step 4 fails):
  Undo step 3: Refund payment (PaymentService)
  Undo step 2: Release inventory reservation (InventoryService)
  Undo step 1: Cancel order (OrderService) → status: CANCELLED
```

## Orchestration Implementation

```java
@Component
@Slf4j
public class CreateOrderSaga {

    private final InventoryServiceClient inventoryClient;
    private final PaymentServiceClient paymentClient;
    private final ShippingServiceClient shippingClient;
    private final OrderRepository orderRepository;

    @Transactional
    public void execute(CreateOrderCommand command) {
        Order order = null;
        boolean inventoryReserved = false;
        PaymentResult paymentResult = null;

        try {
            // Step 1: Create order
            order = createOrder(command);
            log.info("Saga step 1 complete: order {} created", order.getId());

            // Step 2: Reserve inventory
            inventoryClient.reserve(command.getItems(), order.getId());
            inventoryReserved = true;
            log.info("Saga step 2 complete: inventory reserved for order {}", order.getId());

            // Step 3: Process payment
            paymentResult = paymentClient.charge(command.getPaymentDetails(), order.getTotal());
            log.info("Saga step 3 complete: payment {} for order {}",
                paymentResult.getTransactionId(), order.getId());

            // Step 4: Schedule shipping
            shippingClient.schedule(order.getId(), command.getShippingAddress());
            log.info("Saga step 4 complete: shipping scheduled for order {}", order.getId());

            // Step 5: Confirm
            order.setStatus(OrderStatus.CONFIRMED);
            orderRepository.save(order);
            log.info("Saga complete: order {} confirmed", order.getId());

        } catch (ShippingException e) {
            log.error("Saga step 4 failed. Compensating...", e);
            compensate(order, inventoryReserved, paymentResult, command);
        } catch (PaymentDeclinedException e) {
            log.error("Saga step 3 failed (payment declined). Compensating...", e);
            compensate(order, inventoryReserved, null, command);
        } catch (InventoryException e) {
            log.error("Saga step 2 failed (inventory). Compensating...", e);
            compensate(order, false, null, command);
        }
    }

    private void compensate(Order order, boolean inventoryReserved,
                             PaymentResult payment, CreateOrderCommand command) {
        // Compensate in REVERSE order:
        if (payment != null) {
            paymentClient.refund(payment.getTransactionId());
            log.info("Compensation: refunded payment {}", payment.getTransactionId());
        }
        if (inventoryReserved) {
            inventoryClient.releaseReservation(command.getItems(), order.getId());
            log.info("Compensation: inventory released for order {}", order.getId());
        }
        if (order != null) {
            order.setStatus(OrderStatus.CANCELLED);
            orderRepository.save(order);
            log.info("Compensation: order {} cancelled", order.getId());
        }
    }
}
```

## Choreography via Events

```java
// OrderService — emits event, doesn't know who handles it:
@Service
public class OrderService {
    public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest request) {
        Order order = orderRepository.save(buildOrder(request));
        // Publish via Kafka/RabbitMQ:
        eventPublisher.publish(new OrderPlacedEvent(order.getId(), order.getItems()));
        return order;
    }
}

// InventoryService — reacts to event:
@Component
public class InventoryEventHandler {
    @KafkaListener(topics = "order-placed")
    public void handleOrderPlaced(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
        try {
            inventoryService.reserve(event.getItems(), event.getOrderId());
            eventPublisher.publish(new InventoryReservedEvent(event.getOrderId()));
        } catch (InventoryException e) {
            eventPublisher.publish(new InventoryReservationFailedEvent(event.getOrderId()));
        }
    }

    // Handle compensation event if payment fails later:
    @KafkaListener(topics = "payment-failed")
    public void handlePaymentFailed(PaymentFailedEvent event) {
        inventoryService.releaseReservation(event.getOrderId());
    }
}
// PaymentService similarly listens to InventoryReservedEvent, etc.
```

## Outbox Pattern — Reliable Event Publishing

```java
// Problem: saving order and publishing event can fail independently
// Solution: save event to same DB in same transaction, publish asynchronously

@Transactional
public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest request) {
    Order order = orderRepository.save(buildOrder(request));

    // Save event to outbox table IN SAME TRANSACTION:
    OutboxEvent outboxEvent = OutboxEvent.builder()
        .aggregateId(order.getId().toString())
        .eventType("OrderPlaced")
        .payload(objectMapper.writeValueAsString(new OrderPlacedEvent(order)))
        .status(OutboxEvent.Status.PENDING)
        .build();
    outboxRepository.save(outboxEvent);

    return order;
    // Both order and outbox event saved atomically
    // Separate scheduler picks up pending events and publishes to Kafka
}

// Outbox publisher (runs every few seconds):
@Scheduled(fixedDelay = 5000)
@Transactional
public void publishPendingEvents() {
    List<OutboxEvent> pending = outboxRepository.findByStatus(PENDING);
    for (OutboxEvent event : pending) {
        try {
            kafkaTemplate.send(event.getEventType(), event.getPayload());
            event.setStatus(PUBLISHED);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            event.incrementRetryCount();
        }
        outboxRepository.save(event);
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

Sagas are complex — only use them when you actually need cross-service consistency. Many distributed transaction problems can be avoided by better service design (fewer cross-service operations) or by accepting eventual consistency and handling it at the UI layer ("your order is being processed"). When you do need sagas, prefer orchestration for complex flows (easier to visualize and debug) and choreography for simple reactive flows. Always implement the outbox pattern for event publishing — it's the difference between "at-least-once delivery" and data loss.
